    "updated_at": "2025-10-10T07:30:30Z",
    "body": "Yes, so at the time `decode()`'s microtask runs, either the UA obtains images immediately, in which case it would already have queued and ran the microtask to update the image data, switching the request's `state` to `\"broken\"`, either it obtains it on demand, and, _I suppose_, `decode()` will act as such a \"demand\", so we'd end up in the third step that asks to wait until the request is modified, which will be in the next microtask.  \n\nAs for the overhead of running a microtask only to update the request's `state`, it seems it could be optimized away by the implementations if it's not observable to users whether an image's request is in a broken or unavailable state, and while you shouldn't take my word for it, I couldn't find a way to observe it.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
